00:13:43.500 --> 00:13:47.340
It's just a wooden Lazy Susan on the edge of our kitchen island.
它只是放在我们厨房岛台边缘的一个木制旋转托盘。

309
00:13:47.420 --> 00:13:53.420
And I put it there years ago to try and solve the small problem of glasses and water bottles being all over the house.
几年前我把它放在那里，是为了尝试解决水杯和水瓶散落在屋子各处的小问题。

310
00:13:53.660 --> 00:13:56.200
Kids would leave bottles and cups on random surfaces.
孩子们会把瓶子和杯子随手丢在各种地方。

311
00:13:56.200 --> 00:14:01.100
They'd be in different places in the kitchen, which made the kitchen feel visually cluttered.
它们会出现在厨房的不同位置，这让厨房在视觉上显得杂乱无章。

312
00:14:01.100 --> 00:14:05.780
It was a small problem, but an annoying one.
这是一个小问题，但却很烦人。

313
00:14:05.940 --> 00:14:14.180
So I thought that a great way to maybe solve this small problem would be with a small solution of one place where the cups and water bottles go.
所以我认为，解决这个小问题的一个好办法，就是提供一个小小的解决方案：一个专门放置杯子和水瓶的地方。

314
00:14:14.600 --> 00:14:15.460
They have a holding pen.
它们有了一个“收容所”。

315
00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:18.980
And unless they're being used, that's where they live all together.
除非正在使用，否则它们就都待在那里。

316
00:14:19.160 --> 00:14:20.960
It's like one big beverage family.
就像一个大家庭聚在一起。

317
00:14:20.960 --> 00:14:25.340
Finally, the drink spinner works beautifully now.
最终，这个饮料转盘现在运行得非常完美。

318
00:14:25.720 --> 00:14:30.540
It took a while for kids to remember, but it has become second nature to everyone in our house.
孩子们花了一段时间才记住，但现在这已经成了我们家每个人的第二天性。

319
00:14:30.740 --> 00:14:33.060
Your cups and water bottles, they live on the drink spinner.
你的杯子和水瓶，它们就住在饮料转盘上。

320
00:14:33.520 --> 00:14:34.020
End of story.
故事结束。

321
00:14:34.720 --> 00:14:40.800
If I walked into my kitchen and I noticed that drink spinner as I'm looking at my life and being like,
如果我走进厨房，当我审视自己的生活并注意到那个饮料转盘时，我会想：

322
00:14:40.840 --> 00:14:41.660
you know what, what's working?
你知道吗，什么东西是有效的？

323
00:14:41.780 --> 00:14:43.000
Let's celebrate what's working.
让我们庆祝一下那些行之有效的事情吧。

324
00:14:43.160 --> 00:14:47.500
I would say this is working because it's all the problem of keeping track of cups,
我会说这个转盘很有效，因为它解决了追踪杯子的问题，

325
00:14:47.500 --> 00:14:52.000
but also of my silent rage about seeing cups everywhere.
也解决了看到到处都是杯子时我内心默默的愤怒。

326
00:14:53.020 --> 00:14:57.120
Let's say I have silent rage about something else that seems to live all over the house.
假设我对其他似乎散落在屋子各处的东西也感到默默愤怒。

327
00:14:58.120 --> 00:15:06.260
Noticing how the drink spinner is working helps me recognize a small solution for a separate small problem.
注意到饮料转盘是如何发挥作用的，能帮助我为另一个小问题找到一个小小的解决方案。

328
00:15:07.400 --> 00:15:12.380
So as you're identifying what's working, that's the first specific question to help you see why it does.
所以，当你确定什么是行之有效的时候，这是第一个具体的问题，能帮助你明白为什么它有效。

329
00:15:12.560 --> 00:15:13.800
What problem does this solve?
这解决了什么问题？

330
00:15:25.010 --> 00:15:27.930
Before we get into the second question, let me remind you again.
在进入第二个问题之前，让我再次提醒你。

331
00:15:28.250 --> 00:15:32.290
That you don't have to be precious or orderly or organized with these questions.
你不必对这些问题过于讲究、刻板或条理化。

332
00:15:32.550 --> 00:15:34.370
You don't even have to try to answer any of them.
你甚至不必尝试回答其中的任何一个问题。

333
00:15:34.610 --> 00:15:38.850
They're more for those of you who need a little clarity and trying to identify what's working.
它们更多是为那些需要一点清晰思路、试图找出什么才是行之有效的人准备的。

334
00:15:39.270 --> 00:15:42.070
And we do this anyway, because we care about today.
无论如何我们都会这样做，因为我们在乎今天。

335
00:15:42.530 --> 00:15:45.290
We care about who we are right now in this life we live in.
我们在乎此时此刻在这个生活中真实的自己。

336
00:15:45.890 --> 00:15:49.830
We care about remembering that we do actually have things in life that are working.
我们在乎记住生活中确实有行之有效的事情。

337
00:15:50.110 --> 00:15:56.970
We can find our way out of early January discouragement of all we want to change.
我们可以摆脱一月初那种对所有想改变的事情感到灰心的情绪。

338
00:15:58.250 --> 00:15:59.350
We're going to name what's working.
我们要把行之有效的事情列出来。

339
00:15:59.590 --> 00:16:01.890
So use these questions if they help.
所以，如果这些问题有帮助，就用上它们吧。

340
00:16:02.470 --> 00:16:02.610
All right.
好的。

341
00:16:02.670 --> 00:16:05.350
The second question you might ask is, what do I like about this?
你可能会问的第二个问题是：我喜欢它的哪一点？

342
00:16:06.030 --> 00:16:09.530
Let's keep joy and pleasure and ease in mind here.
在这里，让我们保持快乐、愉悦和轻松的心态。

343
00:16:09.710 --> 00:16:11.710
What do you like about this thing that's working?
关于这个行之有效的东西，你喜欢它的什么？

344
00:16:12.510 --> 00:16:19.450
Taking a lunch break every day works so well for me because it solves the problem of not always slowing down on purpose.
每天午休对我来说非常有效，因为它解决了我不总是刻意放慢节奏的问题。

345
00:16:20.230 --> 00:16:21.550
What do I like about it?
我喜欢它的什么呢？

346
00:16:21.910 --> 00:16:22.890
Well, I like food.
嗯，我喜欢食物。

347
00:16:22.890 --> 00:16:23.570
So there's that.
所以，这就是其中一点。

348
00:16:23.770 --> 00:16:24.570
It's nice that it's lunchtime.
能到午餐时间真好。

349
00:16:24.690 --> 00:16:29.650
But I also like that there are no expectations for me for that lunch hour that I take.
但我还喜欢的一点是，在我休息的那个午餐时间里，没有人对我有什么期待。

350
00:16:29.790 --> 00:16:37.270
I can eat and watch a show or eat and read a book or eat and sit on my porch in silence for a few minutes before going inside to watch a show or read a book.
我可以吃东西看节目，或者吃东西看书，又或者在进去看节目或看书之前，在门廊里安静地坐上几分钟。

351
00:16:37.650 --> 00:16:40.310
I like that I can do what I need to do in the moment.
我喜欢那种能随时做我需要做的事情的感觉。

352
00:16:41.030 --> 00:16:44.330
But whatever I choose is genuinely enjoyable to me.
但无论我选择什么，都是我真心享受的。

353
00:16:44.950 --> 00:16:48.090
Name what you will like about a thing that's working.
说出你喜欢某件行之有效的事情的理由。

354
00:16:48.270 --> 00:16:50.270
Put some meat on its bones as to why.
具体说说为什么喜欢它。

355
00:16:50.270 --> 00:16:53.990
It gives it more staying power and personal enjoyment that way.
这样能让它更持久，也能让你获得更多的个人享受。

356
00:16:54.490 --> 00:16:56.350
Remember, perspective really matters here.
记住，视角在这里真的很重要。

357
00:16:56.630 --> 00:17:02.230
And a third question you might ask to help you understand what's working is, how does this make me feel like myself?
第三个可以帮你理解什么行之有效的问题是：这怎么让我感觉像我自己？

358
00:17:02.850 --> 00:17:04.910
Not everything might, and that's fine.
并非所有事情都能做到这一点，这没关系。

359
00:17:04.910 --> 00:17:14.270
But in order to slowly nurture an integrated life that is rooted in your own humanity and what matters most to you in your season,
但为了慢慢培养一种整合的生活，一种植根于你自身人性以及你当前阶段最重要事物的平衡生活，

360
00:17:14.470 --> 00:17:22.770
it's not a ridiculous thing to consider how your decisions and rhythms and systems make you feel like yourself.
考虑你的决定、节奏和系统如何让你感觉像你自己，这并不荒谬。

361
00:17:23.690 --> 00:17:30.870
You might say, this thing makes me feel calm, which keeps me from going outside of myself in a rage.
你可能会说，这件事让我感到平静，这让我不至于因为愤怒而失控。

362
00:17:31.310 --> 00:17:38.350
This thing makes me feel creative, even though I'm doing something mundane because I'm doing it in a unique way that's working for me.
这件事让我感到有创造力，即使我做的是平凡琐事，因为我以一种适合我的独特方式在做。

363
00:17:38.530 --> 00:17:39.250
You see what I mean?
你明白我的意思吗？

364
00:17:40.010 --> 00:17:44.270
Also, a lot of you often ask me how to figure out what makes you feel like yourself.
另外，你们很多人经常问我如何找出什么让你感觉像你自己。

365
00:17:44.970 --> 00:17:48.590
Sometimes I think we put too much pressure on that question or on the feeling.
有时我觉得我们在这个问题或这种感觉上施加了太大的压力。

366
00:17:49.470 --> 00:17:57.310
When something makes you feel like yourself, it might not be the most happy, fulfilled, unicorns and rainbows version of yourself.
当某件事让你感觉像你自己时，它可能并不是那个最快乐、最满足、充满独角兽和彩虹般完美的你。

367
00:17:57.490 --> 00:18:02.250
You might feel like yourself because of the house rule, no piles on the kitchen island.
你可能会因为家规——厨房岛台上不能堆杂物——而感觉像你自己。

368
00:18:02.950 --> 00:18:12.610
Visual clutter might take you out of yourself, so visual tidiness, especially in commonly used spaces like the kitchen, makes you feel more connected to yourself.
视觉上的杂乱可能会让你心烦意乱，所以视觉上的整洁，尤其是在厨房等常用空间，会让你感觉与自己更贴近。

369
00:18:12.850 --> 00:18:15.110
It doesn't have to be technicolor to count.
它不必非得五彩斑斓才算数。

370
00:18:16.010 --> 00:18:24.490
So three questions that could help give a little insight into why something is working are, what problem is this solving?
所以，三个有助于深入了解为什么某件事行之有效的问题是：它解决了什么问题？

371
00:18:25.170 --> 00:18:26.830
What do I like about this?
我喜欢它的什么？

372
00:18:27.210 --> 00:18:29.130
And how does this make me feel like myself?
以及，它怎么让我感觉像我自己？

373
00:18:29.850 --> 00:18:35.150
Now, you might be ready to start naming this in your life, but you might feel a little scattershot about it.
现在，你可能准备好开始在生活中列举这些了，但你可能会觉得有点杂乱无章。

374
00:18:35.250 --> 00:18:42.730
You know, you're a pinball within the machine of your life, just like freely associating what's working as one idea pings into another.
你知道，你就像生活机器里的弹珠，只是自由地联想什么行之有效，一个想法撞向另一个想法。

375
00:18:42.910 --> 00:18:44.650
And that's honestly a great way to do it.
老实说，这是一种很棒的方法。

376
00:18:45.010 --> 00:18:47.270
This does not have to be a detailed homework assignment.
这不必是一项详细的家庭作业。

377
00:18:47.570 --> 00:18:50.010
It's ultimately, it's just a posture.
归根结底，这只是一种心态。

378
00:18:50.630 --> 00:18:51.870
Just name what's working.
只需说出什么行之有效即可。

379
00:18:52.090 --> 00:18:53.010
Look for what's working.
去寻找那些行之有效的事情。

380
00:18:53.290 --> 00:18:55.710
Don't focus so hard on what's not.
不要太纠结于那些行不通的事情。

381
00:18:56.150 --> 00:18:57.210
So ping away.
所以，尽管去碰撞吧。

382
00:18:57.410 --> 00:18:58.690
If you want to ping, ping.
如果你想碰撞，那就碰撞吧。

383
00:18:59.170 --> 00:19:09.070
Now, if you'd like to be a bit more methodical or you would enjoy a new way of looking at the areas in your life so you don't miss anything, even though you totally will,
现在，如果你想更有条理一点，或者你想用一种新的方式来看待你生活中的各个领域，这样你就不会错过任何事情（尽管你肯定会错过一些），

384
00:19:09.490 --> 00:19:11.870
here are three categories that you could consider.
这里有三个你可以考虑的类别。

385
00:19:12.150 --> 00:19:17.970
Time of day, regular events, and rooms in your house.
一天中的时间、常规事件，以及你家里的房间。

386
00:19:18.490 --> 00:19:20.070
Now, these are broad on purpose.
这些类别设定得很宽泛，是有意为之的。

387
00:19:20.070 --> 00:19:27.990
They're simply doors you can walk through to kind of help you notice and narrow your focus on what you would like to notice.
它们只是你可以穿过的门，帮助你注意到并缩小你想要关注的范围。

388
00:19:28.390 --> 00:19:30.690
So category one, the time of day.
所以第一类，一天中的时间。

389
00:19:31.030 --> 00:19:31.170
All right.
好的。

390
00:19:31.450 --> 00:19:34.210
Mornings, mid-morning, if you're home with tiny humans.
早晨，上午，如果你和年幼的孩子待在家里的话。

391
00:19:34.610 --> 00:19:36.950
After school, transitioning from work to home.
放学后，从工作到家庭的过渡。

392
00:19:37.490 --> 00:19:40.670
Dinner, bedtime routine, evenings, et cetera, et cetera.
晚餐、睡前流程、晚上等等，等等。

393
00:19:41.270 --> 00:19:45.030
Start your thinking at one time of day and just look around.
从一天中的某个时间开始思考，环顾四周。

394
00:19:45.690 --> 00:19:47.070
You can even live in it.
你甚至可以亲身去体验。

395
00:19:47.370 --> 00:19:50.290
Like tomorrow morning, pay attention to what's working.
比如明天早上，留意一下什么行之有效。

396
00:19:50.490 --> 00:19:52.450
What do you see that's solving a problem?
你看到了什么正在解决问题？

397
00:19:52.770 --> 00:19:54.250
What do you see that you like?
你看到了什么你喜欢的？

398
00:19:54.490 --> 00:19:59.010
What do you see that makes you feel like yourself or makes someone else in your home feel like themselves?
你看到了什么让你感觉像你自己，或者让你家里其他人感觉像他们自己？

399
00:19:59.850 --> 00:20:05.990
Being present at a certain time of day or using your imagination and memory for that particular time of day,
在一天中的特定时间保持专注，或者利用你的想象力和记忆力来回想那个特定的时间，

400
00:20:05.990 --> 00:20:08.510
it can help you notice what's working.
这可以帮助你注意到什么行之有效。

401
00:20:09.550 --> 00:20:12.310
Category two, regular events.
第二类，常规事件。

402
00:20:12.530 --> 00:20:16.250
What tasks or events or activities happen on a regular basis?
有哪些任务、事件或活动是定期发生的？

403
00:20:16.750 --> 00:20:17.350
Meals.
用餐。

404
00:20:17.490 --> 00:20:18.930
Meals are very regular.
用餐是非常规律的。

405
00:20:19.450 --> 00:20:21.210
Carpool or commuting.
拼车或通勤。

406
00:20:21.690 --> 00:20:22.770
Regular meetings.
定期会议。

407
00:20:23.390 --> 00:20:28.770
Decide once gatherings, like messaging friends while you're waiting for your groceries to be loaded into your car,
决定一次性的聚会，比如在等待杂货装车时给朋友发消息，

408
00:20:28.970 --> 00:20:33.190
or like actually going grocery shopping with a friend every Tuesday or whatever it is.
或者像每周二和朋友一起去买菜之类的活动。

409
00:20:33.330 --> 00:20:35.470
Look on your calendar if you use one.
如果你有使用日历的习惯，可以查看一下。

410
00:20:35.990 --> 00:20:41.070
Or just pay attention to the regular things in life and notice what's working in there.
或者只是留意生活中那些规律的事情，看看哪些地方进展顺利。

411
00:20:41.790 --> 00:20:45.470
And then category three is rooms in your house or rooms in your life.
第三类是你家里的房间，或者你生活中的空间。

412
00:20:45.830 --> 00:20:47.210
Different rooms have different functions.
不同的房间有不同的功能。

413
00:20:47.510 --> 00:20:51.770
So literally walking into a room in your home, it can help you notice what's working there.
所以，走进家里的一个房间，真的可以帮你发现那里有什么是进展顺利的。

414
00:20:52.230 --> 00:20:57.310
There could be decorating decisions or cozy decisions that make you really happy.
可能是一些装饰上的决定或营造舒适感的决定，让你感到非常开心。

415
00:20:58.150 --> 00:21:02.390
Maybe you think of tasks that are often done in that particular room.
也许你会想到在这个特定房间里经常完成的任务。

416
00:21:02.870 --> 00:21:05.350
You know, what about cleaning that specific room?
比如，打扫那个特定的房间怎么样？

417
00:21:05.350 --> 00:21:10.390
Walk into a room in your house, pause, and ask yourself, what's working in here?
走进你家里的一个房间，停下来，问问自己：这里有什么是进展顺利的？

418
00:21:10.930 --> 00:21:13.130
What happens in here that's working?
这里发生了什么进展顺利的事情？

419
00:21:13.310 --> 00:21:14.790
What do I like about this room?
我喜欢这个房间的什么地方？

420
00:21:15.150 --> 00:21:17.310
What do I like about what happens in this room?
我喜欢在这个房间里发生的什么事情？

421
00:21:18.090 --> 00:21:28.630
These can be physical rooms in your own home, but they can also be like your car or your desk at work or any physical space you spend time in.
这些可以是你自己家里的实体房间，也可以是你的车、工作台或任何你花时间待的物理空间。

422
00:21:28.630 --> 00:21:29.990
What's working here?
这里有什么进展顺利的？

423
00:21:30.650 --> 00:21:34.750
So those are the three rules, three questions, and three categories.
以上就是三个规则、三个问题和三个类别。

424
00:21:34.970 --> 00:21:36.970
The three rules are the smaller, the better.
这三个规则是：越小越好。

425
00:21:37.790 --> 00:21:39.430
Working doesn't mean perfect.
进展顺利并不意味着完美。

426
00:21:39.690 --> 00:21:41.290
And now isn't forever.
而且现在并不代表永远。

427
00:21:42.150 --> 00:21:44.810
When you're naming what's working, keep those three things in mind.
当你列举进展顺利的事情时，请记住这三点。

428
00:21:45.450 --> 00:21:51.270
The three questions that might help you identify why something is working are, what problem is this solving?
有助于你找出某件事为何进展顺利的三个问题是：它解决了什么问题？

429
00:21:51.710 --> 00:21:53.070
What do I like about this?
我喜欢它的什么地方？

430
00:21:53.410 --> 00:21:55.110
And how does this make me feel like myself?
它如何让我感觉像我自己？

431
00:21:55.950 --> 00:22:03.810
And then the three categories that could help you narrow your focus are times of day, regular events, and rooms in your home and life.
而有助于你缩小关注范围的三个类别是：一天中的时间段、常规事件，以及你家和生活中的房间。

432
00:22:03.810 --> 00:22:05.470
So what do you do now?
那么你现在该做什么呢？

433
00:22:05.990 --> 00:22:09.530
The point of these ideas is not to fix things.
这些想法的目的不是为了解决问题。

434
00:22:10.150 --> 00:22:11.450
In fact, it's the opposite.
事实上，恰恰相反。

435
00:22:12.110 --> 00:22:19.030
We are noticing what's working so that we stop the spin out of thinking that everything isn't.
我们关注进展顺利的事情，是为了停止那种认为一切都不对劲的焦虑循环。

436
00:22:19.550 --> 00:22:23.990
This time of year has us in our heads about fresh starts and big changes.
每到这个时节，我们总会纠结于新的开始和重大的改变。

437
00:22:24.330 --> 00:22:28.630
And again, while those are totally fine, begin where you are.
再说一次，虽然这些完全没问题，但请从你当下的位置开始。

438
00:22:29.070 --> 00:22:30.610
Begin with what's working.
从进展顺利的事情开始。

439
00:22:31.130 --> 00:22:31.770
Celebrate it.
庆祝它。

440
00:22:32.050 --> 00:22:32.610
Notice it.
留意它。

441
00:22:32.610 --> 00:22:38.470
But so often when we see how many things might be working, we calm down a little about what isn't.
但通常当我们看到有多少事情进展顺利时，我们对那些不顺利的事情就会冷静一些。

442
00:22:39.110 --> 00:22:47.790
So even though you could, in theory, go through your entire life with the three rules in mind and ask all three of those questions, I don't think that's necessary.
所以，尽管理论上你可以带着这三条规则去审视生活的方方面面，并问出那三个问题，但我认为没必要这样做。

443
00:22:48.050 --> 00:22:49.470
That's not starting small anyway.
那毕竟不是从小处着手。

444
00:22:50.110 --> 00:22:55.890
Pay attention to the area of your life that has the biggest pull for you to make it better.
关注你生活中最想改善的那个领域。

445
00:22:57.070 --> 00:23:01.070
Stay in that place and instead name what's working.
留在那个领域，转而列举出进展顺利的事情。

446
00:23:01.070 --> 00:23:03.330
Be where you are.
活在当下。

447
00:23:03.590 --> 00:23:06.270
Be kind about the things already in place.
善待那些已经就位的事物。

448
00:23:06.690 --> 00:23:12.970
Refresh your perspective on the things in your life that do, in fact, solve your probleMs.
刷新你对生活中那些确实解决了你问题的事务的看法。

449
00:23:13.270 --> 00:23:16.610
Things that you like that make you feel like yourself.
那些你喜欢的、让你感觉像你自己的事物。

450
00:23:17.330 --> 00:23:20.910
Be who you are and where you are today.
做你自己，活在当下。

451
00:23:20.910 --> 00:23:22.490
You're doing great.
你做得很好。

452
00:23:23.430 --> 00:23:33.250
If you'd like a peek into what's working for me on a really practical level, episode 349 is called 10 Small Changes I'm Glad I Made.
如果你想了解我在实际生活中行之有效的方法，第349期节目叫作《我很高兴做出的10个小改变》。

453
00:23:33.730 --> 00:23:38.510
And then episode 366 is 20 Helpful Decisions I Keep Repeating.
而第366期节目是《我不断重复的20个有益决定》。

454
00:23:38.510 --> 00:23:42.690
Those are practical, practical lists of what's working in my life.
这些都是关于我生活中行之有效之事的实用清单。

455
00:23:42.810 --> 00:23:44.490
And many of them have been working for years.
其中许多方法已经奏效多年了。

456
00:23:44.650 --> 00:23:50.090
The tiny solutions, the small changes, the decisions you make once that still work.
那些微小的解决方案、小小的改变，以及你做过一次却依然有效的决定。

457
00:23:50.330 --> 00:23:55.950
Those are the parts of a steady system that you don't have to keep starting over on.
这些都是稳定系统的一部分，你不需要反复从头开始。

458
00:23:56.510 --> 00:23:57.790
So be where you are.
所以，安于当下吧。

459
00:23:57.950 --> 00:23:59.050
Notice what's happening today.
留意今天正在发生的事情。

460
00:23:59.590 --> 00:24:01.170
Celebrate the changes you've made.
庆祝你已经做出的改变。

461
00:24:01.370 --> 00:24:03.130
The smaller, the better.
越小越好。

462
00:24:03.130 --> 00:24:06.290
Working doesn't mean perfect and now isn't forever.
有效并不意味着完美，现在也不代表永远。

463
00:24:07.030 --> 00:24:08.690
That's how we start a new year.
这就是我们开启新一年的方式。

464
00:24:09.290 --> 00:24:10.590
Let's name what's working.
让我们列举一下哪些方法是有效的。

465
00:24:11.970 --> 00:24:18.850
One thing I know a lot of you do at the end of December and the beginning of January is to reread The Lazy Genius Way.
我知道你们很多人在12月底和1月初都会做的一件事，就是重读《懒人天才之道》（The Lazy Genius Way）。

466
00:24:18.950 --> 00:24:19.970
I mentioned that last week.
我上周提到过这一点。

467
00:24:20.110 --> 00:24:21.270
I love that y'all do that.
我很高兴你们这样做。

468
00:24:21.530 --> 00:24:25.110
It's something that works for you and it's humbling and fun for me.
这对你们有效，对我来说既受宠若惊又很有趣。

469
00:24:25.370 --> 00:24:26.090
So thank you.
所以，谢谢你们。

470
00:24:26.670 --> 00:24:31.670
Having a refresher on the 13 Lazy Genius Principles as you're going into a new year, it really is helpful.
在迈向新一年时，重温这13条“懒人天才原则”真的很有帮助。

471
00:24:31.670 --> 00:24:40.830
If you have never read my first book, The Lazy Genius Way, it dedicates a chapter to each of the 13 Lazy Genius Principles.
如果你从未读过我的第一本书《懒人天才之道》，书中专门用一章来介绍这13条懒人天才原则。

472
00:24:41.010 --> 00:24:55.010
Decide once, start small, ask the magic question, live in the season, build the right routines, set house rules, put everything in its place, let people in, batch it, essentialize,
一次决定、从小事做起、问那个神奇的问题、顺应季节生活、建立合适的日常惯例、设定家庭规则、物归原位、接纳他人、批量处理、精简重点，

473
00:24:55.350 --> 00:24:57.770
go in the right order, and be kind to yourself.
按正确的顺序行事，并善待自己。

474
00:24:58.310 --> 00:25:03.750
It's the little engine that could book, just plugging along, still selling copies over four years later.
这本书就像那本《小火车头》，一直在努力前行，四年多后依然在售。

475
00:25:04.030 --> 00:25:08.350
If you've never read it, but you have thought about it, now is a really good time to do it.
如果你从未读过，但曾考虑过，现在正是阅读它的好时机。

476
00:25:08.650 --> 00:25:09.650
I read the audio book,
我录制了这本书的有声书，

477
00:25:09.730 --> 00:25:17.910
so if that's more your speed to listen as you're like taking down holiday decorations or going for wintry walks or trying to keep your children entertained before school goes back,
所以如果你更喜欢在收拾节日装饰、冬日散步，或者在开学前试图让孩子们保持安静时听书，

478
00:25:18.050 --> 00:25:18.830
give it a shot.
不妨试一试。

479
00:25:18.830 --> 00:25:19.850
I think you'll enjoy it.
我想你会喜欢的。

480
00:25:20.370 --> 00:25:22.810
This is also an excellent time of year to read the plan.
现在也是阅读《计划》（The Plan）这本书的绝佳时机。

481
00:25:23.010 --> 00:25:25.110
It's all about compassionate time management,
它讲的是富有同情心的时间管理，

482
00:25:25.430 --> 00:25:34.410
helping you rewire your thinking from optimization and greatness and making the most out of your time to just being a grounded whole person right where you are.
帮助你重塑思维，从追求优化、卓越和最大化利用时间，转变为就在当下做一个脚踏实地的完整的人。

483
00:25:34.930 --> 00:25:38.850
Being okay with some things being great and others just being mid.
接受有些事情可以很棒，而有些事情平平无奇就好。

484
00:25:39.550 --> 00:25:45.630
It's practical, permission giving, and full of pep talks to get you through any January chaos you might experience.
它很实用，能给你授权，并充满鼓励，帮你度过可能遇到的任何一月混乱。

485
00:25:45.970 --> 00:25:47.310
I read that audio book too.
我也录制了那本有声书。

486
00:25:47.530 --> 00:25:50.970
But I think for the plan, you'll be so glad you have the physical copy.
但我认为对于《计划》这本书，你会很高兴拥有纸质版。

487
00:25:51.130 --> 00:25:53.890
So thanks for reading my books and supporting my work, y'all.
所以，谢谢大家阅读我的书并支持我的工作。

488
00:25:54.010 --> 00:25:54.510
It means a lot.
这对我意义重大。

489
00:25:54.990 --> 00:25:57.870
Okay, before we go, let's celebrate the lazy genius of the week.
好了，在结束之前，让我们来庆祝一下本周的“懒人天才”。

490
00:25:58.070 --> 00:26:07.390
This week, it's Ashley Eidson with a super interesting approach to holiday gift giving that actually matters a little right now at the beginning of the year.
本周是Ashley Eidson，她有一种非常有趣的节日送礼方式，在年初的现在其实很有参考价值。

491
00:26:07.390 --> 00:26:16.050
Ashley writes, every year, my husband and I save up our credit card rewards across a few different cards, and we don't spend them all year.
Ashley写道，每年我和丈夫都会把几张信用卡攒下的积分存起来，全年都不动用。

492
00:26:16.510 --> 00:26:21.350
Then when it is Christmas time, the amount we have in rewards is our budget.
然后到了圣诞节时，我们拥有的积分总额就是我们的预算。

493
00:26:21.590 --> 00:26:23.770
We know how many gifts we need to buy each year.
我们知道每年需要买多少份礼物。

494
00:26:23.930 --> 00:26:26.810
We know how much we have, and that's how we divide it up.
我们知道有多少积分，然后就按此分配。

495
00:26:27.310 --> 00:26:32.310
Some years we've had to make some decisions like not getting gifts for every coworker in the office,
有些年份我们不得不做些取舍，比如不给办公室里的每位同事都买礼物，

496
00:26:32.310 --> 00:26:37.710
but it always seems to work out where the people we want to give gifts to comes very close to our total.
但总能凑巧让我们要送礼的对象与我们的总预算非常接近。

497
00:26:38.030 --> 00:26:44.710
Last year, we had a little bit higher rewards total, and we were able to buy some presents for ourselves at the end of the year on top of everything else we purchased.
去年，我们的积分总额稍微多了一点，除了购买所有计划内的礼物外，我们还能在年底给自己买些礼物。

498
00:26:45.050 --> 00:26:51.430
Hope this helps someone else enjoy the holiday season without stressing about breaking the bank with the regular monthly bills and expenses.
希望这能帮助其他人享受节日季，而不必担心因日常账单和开支而破产。

499
00:26:51.690 --> 00:26:53.130
What a fantastic idea this is.
这真是个绝妙的主意。

500
00:26:53.510 --> 00:26:56.810
Can you see why I wanted to share this one like right now at the start of the year?
你能明白为什么我想在年初的现在分享这个故事了吗？

501
00:26:56.810 --> 00:27:00.630
This could be a fantastic approach for a lot of us when it comes to holiday budgets.
对于我们很多人来说，这可能是处理假期预算的一种绝佳方法。

502
00:27:00.790 --> 00:27:02.770
The rewards just do it for you.
奖励机制会帮你搞定一切。

503
00:27:03.090 --> 00:27:04.670
Love how practical and helpful this is.
很喜欢这种既实用又有帮助的方法。

504
00:27:04.790 --> 00:27:08.830
So thank you for sharing, Ashley, and congratulations on being the Lazy Genius of the week.
所以谢谢你的分享，Ashley，也恭喜你成为本周的“懒人天才”。

505
00:27:08.990 --> 00:27:14.950
This episode is hosted by me, Kendra Adachi, and executive produced by Kendra Adachi, Jenna Fisher, and Angela Kinsey.
本期节目由我 Kendra Adachi 主持，并由 Kendra Adachi、Jenna Fisher 和 Angela Kinsey 担任执行制作人。

506
00:27:15.250 --> 00:27:19.330
The Lazy Genius podcast is enthusiastically part of the Office Ladies Network.
“懒人天才”播客非常荣幸地加入了 Office Ladies 网络。

507
00:27:19.570 --> 00:27:21.830
Special thanks to Leah Jarvis for weekly production.
特别感谢 Leah Jarvis 的每周制作。

508
00:27:22.230 --> 00:27:23.150
Thanks, y'all, for listening.
感谢大家的收听。

509
00:27:23.150 --> 00:27:27.590
And until next time, be a genius about the things that matter and lazy about the things that don't.
下次见，愿你在重要的事情上做个天才，在不重要的事情上做个懒人。

510
00:27:27.670 --> 00:27:29.370
I'm Kendra, and I'll see you next week.
我是 Kendra，我们下周见。
